we here at CBS News are committed to  stopping the stigma surrounding mental  health in taking the shame and the blame  out of discussing mental illness so this  morning we're hearing from four people  whose children died by suicide suicide  is the second leading cause of death  among teenagers in this country a recent  study found that teen suicide jumped 56  percent from 2007 to 2010 two health  experts say bullying and social media  have both contributed to the rise our  national correspondent that's Jericka  Duncan spoke to these four parents and  so jurika what do they hope to  accomplish by speaking up very glad that  they are what do they want to accomplish  they really want to help others that's  what all four of them said it comes down  to helping other people in our  conversation with these four parents we  saw incredible strength in the midst of  grief and each had a message of  awareness in Russell believe social  media is partly responsible for the  death of his 14 year old daughter Molly  Freddy Aviles 13 year old daughter  Rosalie died by suicide after he says  she experienced extreme bullying in  school kaamika Shelby's 15 year old son  Nigel died earlier this year after  coming out as gay and Carolyn's a no  lost her 18 year old son Cameron to  suicide in 2005 after he battled years  of depression and addiction it was such  a joy he was always being silly he loved  to dance he looked the same  roley's she was an angel she wanted to  be a lawyer and I asked her why do you  want to be a lawyer because I won't make  the world a better place  Cameron he was a very good artist  photographer generally just a very sweet  caring kid molly was adorable one of the  most caring people that I've ever known  and she was obviously also very good at  keeping secrets because she managed to  keep from us as a family that she was  suffering in this way we had no idea  statistics showed that in nine out of  ten suicides the person had diagnoseable  mental health issue